Dear Mr. McClure,
Thank you for your email, we always appreciate feedback that helps us to
improve our programs for you and your children. On behalf of our team, I
apologize for the uncomfortable situation last Friday night at Tinker Time.
I completely agree that this type of behavior is not appropriate and assure
you that it is not allowed from our team. They are committed to providing a
rewarding experience for all of our participants.

I did clarify that Becca and Gina were the only STEM Action Center
employees at Tinker Time and they were not the individuals having the
conversations. Becca and Gina were engaged with helping other participants
and unaware of these conversations. However, Becca is fairly certain that
she knows who was probably engaging in the conversations and she will reach
out and let them know that they need to be more mindful of the
family-friendly environment. We will also post a sign that reminds
participants that we are a family-friendly organization and that they need
to keep their conversations appropriate and relevant to the amazing maker
learning experience that we strive to provide for everyone.

Again, thank you for bringing this to our attention and I hope that we will
see you soon!
Warm regards,
Tami

-- 
*Tamara L. Goetz, Ph.D.*
*Director*
*Utah STEM Action Center*
*Department of Cultural and Community Engagement*
3848 S West Temple, Suite 105
Salt Lake City, UT 84115
Ph: 801-674-2405

